<!--
    Tests/Editor compile tripwire (issue #193). No CI lane compiled
    Tests/Editor, so a test that could not compile (a missing using, a
    renamed member) landed green until a live editor ran it. This project
    compiles the real Runtime sources plus every Tests/Editor source
    against the same pinned UnityEngine 2021.3.33 reference assemblies
    the package minimum (and runtime-2021.3.csproj) pins, so a broken
    test fails the build outright.

    UnityEngine.TestRunner (LogAssert) is not part of the
    UnityEngine.Modules package; LogAssertCompileStub.cs next to this
    file stands in for the members the suite calls. The stub is
    compile-only: the two files that call LogAssert run on a live
    editor, never here.

    Stated limit: `#if UNITY_EDITOR` regions compile nowhere in CI - the
    pin carries no UnityEditor assemblies, and the host deliberately
    leaves UNITY_EDITOR undefined. The guarded test code (the discovery
    fixtures' Reflection.Emit rig, the inclusion tests' editor-only
    cases) is editor-answered only; the compile tripwire covers
    unguarded code.

    Execution of the engine-independent fixtures is testhost/'s job;
    this project only answers "does it compile against 2021.3".

    The project lives in its own directory so its obj/bin never touch
    another project's: runtime-2021.3.csproj sat beside this file and
    both restored into one tooling~/compat/obj, where concurrent
    restores scrambled each other's project.assets.json into a CS0246
    storm (measured: a 1,294-error build that passes either build
    alone). Directory isolation is what lets the compile gate run
    concurrently with the runtime build instead of serialized behind
    it - setting BaseIntermediateOutputPath in the project body fires
    after Microsoft.Common.props resolved the extensions path, so the
    restore still collided (measured: MSB3539 + a 2,724-error NUnit
    storm under concurrency).

    Run via `dotnet build tooling~/compat/tests/tests-2021.3.csproj`, or
    as part of `npm run test:editor`.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.1</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
        CS0649/CS0169/CS0414: Unity assigns serialized fields outside C#, so
        a Unity-free compile sees them as never assigned/used.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1701;CS0649;CS0169;CS0414</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="UnityEngine.Modules" Version="2021.3.33" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit" Version="3.14.0" />
    <Compile Include="../../../Runtime/**/*.cs" Exclude="../../../Runtime/Analyzers/**" />
    <Compile Include="../../../Tests/Editor/**/*.cs" />
    <Compile Include="LogAssertCompileStub.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);ENABLE_LEGACY_INPUT_MANAGER</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
